{
  "name": "AKU alerts government to HIV/AIDS outbreak in children ",
  "parent_id": 203314470211128,
  "group_id": "25a759bd-36c1-484e-bc6e-05a61f8daf99",
  "alternates": [],
  "created_at": "2026-07-31T09:24:26.712Z",
  "deleted_at": null,
  "sort_by_date": null,
  "tag_list": [],
  "updated_at": "2026-07-31T09:24:26.795Z",
  "published_at": "2026-07-31T09:24:26.781Z",
  "id": 203981892494721,
  "uuid": "4c6e3367-9b25-4172-a016-0ef6358ca0c9",
  "is_folder": false,
  "content": {
    "_uid": "3f36f136-774a-486c-007e-277d4ddd26cf",
    "body": [
      {
        "_uid": "1667ae3c-2ba3-1561-b9cf-0e5cdf7c089a",
        "content": {
          "type": "doc",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "content": [
                {
                  "text": "With funding from the World Health Organization, Associate Professor Syed Hani Abidi and colleagues studied the outbreak using phylogenetic analysis. They found that the HIV strains involved (known as CRF02_AG and A1) likely originated among people who inject drugs. Nationally, around 40 per cent of adult intravenous drug users have HIV/AIDS, and Larkana has a significant population of people who inject drugs. ",
                  "type": "text"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "content": [
                {
                  "text": "Poor infection control practices in health facilities then allowed the strains circulating among drug users to be transmitted to children. While initial media reports implicated a single local HIV-positive doctor in the outbreak, AKU researchers found multiple clusters within the outbreak, indicating a number of healthcare facilities contributed to the spread of the virus. That suggests that poor infection prevention practices are widespread. ",
                  "type": "text"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "content": [
                {
                  "text": "Today, 98 per cent of the infected children are taking antiretroviral drugs and should be able to lead long and generally healthy lives. That is partly thanks to AKU. University staff assisted with screening the population, opening a new clinic for HIV-positive children, treating hundreds of patients in Larkana and training health professionals in managing patients with HIV/AIDS. Yet much remains to be done, according to Associate Professor Fatima Mir, lead author of the Lancet article.",
                  "type": "text"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "content": [
                {
                  "text": "\"It's unconscionable that these children were infected,\" Dr Mir said. \"The government response to the outbreak has been commendable, but now we need to see a major effort to improve infection control in both the public and private sectors.\"",
                  "type": "text"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "content": [
                {
                  "text": "Study co-investigators included Associate Professor Syed Faisal Mahmood and Associate Professor Rehana Siddiqui.",
                  "type": "text"
                },
                {
                  "type": "hard_break"
                },
                {
                  "type": "hard_break"
                }
              ]
            },
